          %h2#Definition Definition
          %p
            The following definition of the degree of a polynomial is formulated vis the
            %em
              formal
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivative"} derivative
            or
            %em left shift
            of a polynomial.
          %p
            The idea behind this lies in the traditional conception of polynomials as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sums"} sums
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomials"} monomials
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplication"} products
            of non-zero
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalars"} scalars
            with powers of an “indeterminate” – which have an associated exponent because commutative rings are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power-associative"} power-associative
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplication"} multiplication
          %p
            Both the derivative and the left shift are functions on polynomials which reduce the exponent of the associated indeterminate by one and take polynomials, relative to the given indeterminate, to zero. Since both operations are also
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+functions"} linear functions
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero"} zero
          %p
            This allows us to define the degree of a polynomial using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} induction
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            and on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lists"} lists
            of natural numbers, as well as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            of left shifts, without having to write out long formulae using indices and ellipses everywhere in the definition, and without having to include additional structure with each polynomial
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            in the form of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar"} scalar
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %h3#polynomials_in_one_indeterminate Polynomials in one indeterminate
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomials"} polynomials
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R[x]
            in one indeterminate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial
            commutative ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R[x]
            with an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in R[x]
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+homomorphism"} ring homomorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mi R
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h:R \to R[x]
          %h4#using_derivatives Using derivatives
          %p
            The
            %strong formal derivative
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{d (-)}{d x}:R[x] \to R[x]
            is a function which is inductively defined on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R[x]
            by
          %ul
            %li
              Constants go to zero:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi h
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi r
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{d h(r)}{d x} = 0
              for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \in R
            %li
              Indeterminate goes to one:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{d x}{d x} = h(1)
            %li
              Addition preservation:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo +
                        %mi q
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi p
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi q
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{d (p + q)}{d x} = \frac{d p}{d x} + \frac{d q}{d x}
              for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in R[x]
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q \in R[x]
            %li
              Leibniz rule:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mi q
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi q
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi x
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi q
                    %mo +
                    %mi p
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi q
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{d (p \cdot q)}{d x} = \frac{d q}{d x} \cdot q + p \cdot \frac{d q}{d x}
              for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in R[x]
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q \in R[x]
          %p
            By the universal property of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
